<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Cleartext HTTP is what the mesh speaks.

     Every node serves its administration API, its snapshot and its two video streams over plain
     HTTP on port 47180: core has no TLS listener there, and the cluster is a private LAN segment
     whose traffic is authenticated by the cluster PSK rather than by transport security.

     This file used to carry a base-config with cleartextTrafficPermitted="false" and an exception
     for loopback only. That silently broke every peer request on the modern tier: from API 24 a
     Network Security Config overrides the android:usesCleartextTraffic="true" that the modern
     flavour manifest declares, so http://&lt;peer-ip&gt;:47180/... raised
     "Cleartext HTTP traffic to &lt;ip&gt; not permitted" inside HttpURLConnection. The door tiles
     swallowed it as an empty still and nothing reached logcat. legacy19 never saw it, because
     API 19 predates the policy and permits cleartext outright, so the two tiers disagreed about
     whether the product worked at all.

     A Network Security Config cannot express "the local subnet": domain-config takes hostnames,
     and peer addresses are DHCP addresses that change. Permitting cleartext is therefore the
     honest declaration of what this app does, and it now matches the modern manifest rather than
     contradicting it. Certificate trust stays at the platform default, so anything this app does
     speak over HTTPS still validates normally. -->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"/>
</network-security-config>
